            <div id="legacybiz">
                <p class="subheading"> Legacy Businesses </p>
                <p> Legacy businesses are places that have been operating for more than 30 years and contribute
                    historical and cultural significance to the identity of San Francisco, as designated by the SF 
                    Government. These businesses, as well as the countless other businesses not formally 
                    recognized by the registry (available 
                    <a href="https://sfgov.maps.arcgis.com/apps/dashboards/471cc5798f8c486fb0f8e9ac8badaf03#"> here</a>)
                    contribute significantly to the identity that builds our local communities. Located all over 
                    the city, these businesses' purposes vary widely, from art galleries to restaurants, but all 
                    are connected by their meaning to the city as a whole. 
                </p>
            </div>

            <div id="HUNs" class="info_block">
                <p class="subheading"> Historically Underserved Neighborhoods (HUNs) </p>
                <p> Historically Underserved Neighborhoods (HUNs) are neighborhoods and areas in San 
                    Francisco that have a larger percentage of lower-income people, and often lack the 
                    same resources and opportunities that other neighborhoods get. Examples of these areas 
                    include the Bayview, with high African American, Hispanic, and Asian populations, as well 
                    as the Tenderloin and Western Addition. Many, if not all of these underserved regions in 
                    SF, have undergone the historical process of redlining in the 1940s by the Home Owners' 
                    Loan Corporation (HOLC), which created significant differences in the housing situation of 
                    citizens as well as their access to loans, which helped create the differences we see in 
                    different neighborhoods today. If you'd like to read more about redlining, please go 
                    <a href= "information#redlining"> 
                    to this relevant subheading. </a>
                </p>
            </div>

